WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.060
And this gets back to this
idea that we deliberately learn

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:05.130
things and I don't believe that.

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:07.080
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here.

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:11.880
Today I wanna talk about
reading and fluency.

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:15.660
Now remember, if you enjoy
these videos, please subscribe.

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:17.100
I talk about language learning.

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:20.670
Hopefully encourage people to
learn languages, share some of my

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:22.680
experience in learning languages.

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:25.320
So if you enjoy them, please do subscribe.

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:27.010
Click on the bell for notifications.

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:31.049
Uh, if you follow me on a, uh, podcast
service, please leave a comment.

00:00:31.049 --> 00:00:32.040
I always appreciate it.

00:00:32.519 --> 00:00:38.580
So, the reason I'm talking about reading
in fluency is because, as you know, if you

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:44.010
follow me on this channel, I very much,
you know, support the idea of reading

00:00:44.015 --> 00:00:51.045
and listening as sort of the principal
means of uh, growing and improving

00:00:51.105 --> 00:00:53.055
in a language that we are learning.

00:00:53.205 --> 00:00:57.405
So I was asked on, uh, by one of the
commenters here, uh, on my YouTube

00:00:57.410 --> 00:01:02.895
channel, uh, about my thoughts about,
uh, a video put out by a certain Hadar

00:01:03.255 --> 00:01:07.365
who has a channel called, um, The Accents
Way or something like that, I'll leave

00:01:07.365 --> 00:01:12.045
a link in the description box, where
she ismore sort of in favor of speaking.

00:01:13.059 --> 00:01:18.820
And, uh, suggest that there is a, a
sort of, um, if all we're ever doing

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:24.190
is reading, we're avoiding, we're
avoiding the challenge of speaking.

00:01:24.910 --> 00:01:30.759
And I, I don't see that the one in any way
that reading prevents you from speaking.

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:36.100
Uh, and I don't think that while we
are reading that we are necessarily

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:41.565
escaping from the obvious benefits
of speaking the language whenever

00:01:41.565 --> 00:01:43.065
we have an opportunity to speak.

00:01:43.274 --> 00:01:48.975
So to what extent is reading
connected with the idea of fluency?

00:01:49.184 --> 00:01:52.274
Because obviously we all
want to achieve fluency.

00:01:52.335 --> 00:01:53.445
I, I shouldn't say we all, but.

00:01:53.830 --> 00:01:58.179
For many people, a primary goal of
language learning is to achieve fluency.

00:01:58.270 --> 00:01:59.740
It doesn't have to be the goal.

00:01:59.830 --> 00:02:04.210
And I think this is also very important,
we learn languages for our own reasons.

00:02:04.539 --> 00:02:07.870
If we want to learn just enough
of the language to be able to feel

00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:11.950
comfortable in Mexico on our next
visit, for example, that's legitimate.

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:16.360
You might be disappointed that there
are, there is no shortcut to being

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:19.299
comfortable in the language, but at
least if you have some knowledge of the

00:02:19.299 --> 00:02:23.370
language you can, you know, have some
communication with the locals, let's

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:24.960
say in Spanish if you're in Mexico.

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:29.340
So that could be a goal, or the goal could
be simply to read books in the language.

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:30.720
That can be a goal.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:33.990
But I think for most people they
would like to achieve fluency.

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:35.910
So what is fluency?

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:40.500
To me, and I said this before
and I'll flash up on the

00:02:40.505 --> 00:02:43.380
screen, B2 in the European...

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:47.430
common European Framework of,
sort of, language proficiency.

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:49.799
And we'll see that B2...

00:02:49.890 --> 00:02:54.720
and remember that the Common
European Framework, uh, has sort

00:02:54.720 --> 00:03:00.120
of six levels and that, you know,
A1, A2 is sort of a beginner level.

00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:02.730
B1, B2 is the intermediate level.

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:05.010
C1, C2 is the advanced level.

00:03:05.609 --> 00:03:08.250
It's possible to look at these
and think that there's sort of

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:13.049
an equal distance from A1 to
A2, from A2 to B1 from B1 to B2.

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:14.269
I don't see it that way.

00:03:14.615 --> 00:03:20.885
I think that from A1 to A2 moves along
fairly quickly, uh, and then to get

00:03:20.885 --> 00:03:24.215
from A2 to B2 is a very long road.

00:03:24.515 --> 00:03:28.865
But that B2 level to my
mind represents fluency.

00:03:29.375 --> 00:03:32.525
It means that you're able to
converse on a variety of subjects,

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:35.430
sort of, call it adult subjects.

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:40.500
Sophisticated subjects, complicated
subjects, not nuclear physics, but

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:45.180
some discussion even of politics,
philosophy, economics, your feelings,

00:03:45.269 --> 00:03:50.340
even though you make mistakes, even
though you might not understand certain

00:03:50.345 --> 00:03:54.520
things, you might be missing a word
here and there, but you are communicate.

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:55.450
You are...

00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:56.920
that to me is b2.

00:03:56.980 --> 00:03:58.210
That to me is fluency.

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:00.910
It may be a surprise to some
people to know that you need

00:04:00.910 --> 00:04:02.590
a lot of words to do that.

00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:06.850
You need a large passive vocabulary,
and I stress all the time the

00:04:06.850 --> 00:04:08.380
importance of passive vocabulary.

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:12.010
You cannot have a conversation
with someone, an intelligent,

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:16.000
sophisticated conversation if you
don't understand what they are saying.

00:04:16.090 --> 00:04:21.070
And so you need a lot of words,
a lot of passive vocabulary so

00:04:21.070 --> 00:04:23.950
that you have a good chance of
understanding of what they're saying.

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:25.500
Of understanding what they're saying.

00:04:26.010 --> 00:04:29.640
Uh, similarly, if you have a
good vocabulary, now you can read

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:31.590
books now, you can watch movies.

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:33.060
You can understand podcasts.

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:36.480
You can do a lot of these things,
which will enable you to continue

00:04:36.485 --> 00:04:37.660
to improve in the language.

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:39.540
It all requires comprehension.

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:41.670
It all requires a large vocabulary.

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:47.310
So reading, especially if accompanied
with listening, is a great way

00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:48.780
to increase your vocabulary.

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:54.390
Now, having a large passive vocabulary
doesn't mean that you can use those words.

00:04:54.900 --> 00:05:00.489
And in the video that I watched that
this, this, uh, person Hadar put out,

00:05:00.669 --> 00:05:04.919
she seemed to suggest, you know, if
you come across a new word or a new

00:05:04.919 --> 00:05:09.780
structure in your reading, if you
don't immediately try to use it, if

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:13.500
you don't immediately try to nail it
down, then you aren't gonna learn it.

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:17.070
And this gets back to this
idea that we deliberately learn

00:05:17.070 --> 00:05:19.140
things and I don't believe that.

00:05:19.559 --> 00:05:26.700
I believe that we gradually get, we start
to assimilate and retain and to be able to

00:05:27.069 --> 00:05:33.615
retrieve things that we have met several
times, hopefully in interesting context.

00:05:33.854 --> 00:05:37.635
And all of a sudden we understand
more and more of the language, and

00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:42.104
if we have opportunity to speak,
we end up, uh, starting to activate

00:05:42.104 --> 00:05:43.965
some of that passive vocabulary.

00:05:44.354 --> 00:05:49.695
So that's, it's, it's more of a fuzzy
logic, natural subconscious process

00:05:49.755 --> 00:05:51.885
rather than a deliberate process.

00:05:52.034 --> 00:05:57.225
So, because you need a large
vocabulary to be, sort of, getting

00:05:57.225 --> 00:05:59.265
back to that B2 definition.

00:05:59.355 --> 00:06:04.695
Therefore, a lot of reading is very
important if you want to achieve fluency.

00:06:04.905 --> 00:06:10.335
Now, there are techniques that you
can use so that you are reading

00:06:10.455 --> 00:06:14.055
that to help you retain more of the
words that you're coming across.

00:06:14.355 --> 00:06:17.535
That's why at LingQ we have a system.

00:06:18.375 --> 00:06:21.015
We enable you to save words to a database.

00:06:21.405 --> 00:06:25.965
Uh, you can review the words, uh, you
can, if the text is very difficult,

00:06:25.995 --> 00:06:27.974
you can look at it in sentence mode.

00:06:28.005 --> 00:06:32.940
Uh, if you want to try to find content
that is at that, what I consider

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:38.669
the sweet spot of 10 to 15% unknown
words, some of which may be names, but

00:06:38.669 --> 00:06:42.539
that's gonna be at a level where it's
not too difficult for you to read.

00:06:42.570 --> 00:06:46.799
With the help of the functionality that
we have at LingQ, with the help of audio,

00:06:46.979 --> 00:06:51.549
it's not gonna be too difficult, but you
are exposing yourself to more new words.

00:06:52.905 --> 00:06:57.284
Obviously, uh, another technique is to
stay within a certain, you know, subject

00:06:57.344 --> 00:07:02.555
area, be it economics or politics, or
certain writers who tend to use the

00:07:02.555 --> 00:07:07.230
same vocabulary so that you have a
better chance of coming across these

00:07:07.410 --> 00:07:10.650
relatively low frequency words more often.

00:07:10.650 --> 00:07:15.140
So these are some of the techniques
that you can use to help the,

00:07:15.145 --> 00:07:19.800
the reading activity give you
that potential to become fluent.

00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:24.000
In order to become fluent, you
need quite a large vocabulary.

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:27.690
I don't, you don't need an enormous,
you don't need to know every

00:07:28.140 --> 00:07:29.180
possible word in the diction.

00:07:29.685 --> 00:07:32.625
You can also forget some
of the very basic words.

00:07:32.865 --> 00:07:34.005
That's not a problem.

00:07:34.215 --> 00:07:37.664
But you need to have, and I refer to
this reserve, you need to have this

00:07:37.664 --> 00:07:41.414
large background with the knowledge
you know, of, of these words, or at

00:07:41.414 --> 00:07:45.164
least with the, the, the having had the
experience of, of encountering these

00:07:45.164 --> 00:07:49.695
words so that slowly you start to activate
them when you have a chance to speak.

00:07:49.844 --> 00:07:53.055
The fact, therefore, reading
is not an escape from speaking,

00:07:53.175 --> 00:07:54.825
it's a preparation for speaking.

00:07:55.125 --> 00:07:59.795
If you combine it with listen, then it's
an even better preparation for speaking.

00:08:00.245 --> 00:08:06.275
And, uh, the idea that somehow many
people, uh, and this was the point of

00:08:06.275 --> 00:08:08.855
this video, and you, you can have a
look at it, she seemed to suggest that

00:08:08.855 --> 00:08:13.625
listening to podcast and reading and so
forth, this is, you are, uh, escaping

00:08:13.630 --> 00:08:15.605
from that obligation to go out and use it.

00:08:15.875 --> 00:08:20.375
Yes, it is true that there are
many people who have a good passive

00:08:20.375 --> 00:08:23.645
understanding of let's say English
and who are reluctant to use it.

00:08:23.765 --> 00:08:25.865
Of course, you shouldn't
be reluctant to use it.

00:08:27.090 --> 00:08:31.570
The better your vocabulary, the better
your understanding of the language.

00:08:31.590 --> 00:08:34.980
The better you read, by and
large, the more confidence

00:08:34.980 --> 00:08:36.450
you're going to have in speaking.

00:08:36.930 --> 00:08:40.680
If you don't have that large
passive vocabulary and you miss

00:08:40.680 --> 00:08:43.590
a lot of what's going on around
you, you will be more reluctant

00:08:43.595 --> 00:08:45.960
to speak and to arrive at a flu...

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:51.770
at a level of fluency, that B2 level,
you have to combine a lot of input

00:08:51.770 --> 00:08:56.025
activity, increasing your vocabulary,
increasing your comprehension, and then

00:08:56.025 --> 00:08:58.365
finding every opportunity to speak.

00:08:58.395 --> 00:08:59.325
And to speak a lot.

00:08:59.385 --> 00:09:01.065
To speak well, you have to speak a lot.

00:09:01.065 --> 00:09:02.025
I've said that before.

00:09:02.175 --> 00:09:07.905
However, lots of reading and listening
can also contribute towards pushing

00:09:07.910 --> 00:09:13.740
you towards that level, uh, of fluency,
which most of us aspire to achieve.

00:09:13.860 --> 00:09:17.520
And I'll leave you with a couple of
videos that I've done on the subject

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:21.240
of listening and reading and their
importance to our language learning.

00:09:21.479 --> 00:09:22.290
Thank you for listening.

00:09:22.469 --> 00:09:23.069
Bye for now.

